<body>
<h1>Unit 16: Refraction through Prisms</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Waves &amp; Optics</p><p class="summary">A prism is a device that separates wavelengths as it bends them. The unit connects geometry to spectroscopy: the apex angle sets the deviation, the refractive index sets the dispersion, and the spectrum is the primary chemical and astronomical fingerprint of light.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>A prism deviates light because its faces are not parallel, and it disperses light because the refractive index depends on wavelength - normally n is larger for blue than for red.</li><li>For a thin prism of small apex angle A, the deviation is approximately d = (n - 1) A.</li><li>The dispersive power w = (n_v - n_r)/(n_y - 1) is nearly constant for a given material, which is why glasses can be sorted into crown and flint families.</li><li>Minimum deviation occurs when the ray path is symmetric (i = e), giving n = sin((A + d_m)/2) / sin(A/2) - the standard laboratory method.</li><li>A hollow prism filled with a liquid measures that liquid's refractive index by the same minimum-deviation method.</li><li>The rainbow is refraction plus one internal reflection plus dispersion in water droplets, which is why the primary bow has an angle of about 42 degrees.</li><li>At minimum deviation the ray path is symmetric, so the refractive index can be measured without knowing the prism's apex angle precisely - which is why the spectrometer method is the reference technique.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The thin-prism approximation requires a small apex angle; thick prisms need the exact two-surface treatment with Snell's law applied twice.</li><li>Dispersion is small - the index changes by only about 0.01 to 0.02 across the visible for glass - so a single prism gives a spectrum whose orders overlap and may need a second prism to isolate.</li><li>Deviation depends on the refractive index of the surrounding medium: a prism in water disperses far less than the same prism in air.</li><li>Refractive index depends on temperature as well as wavelength, so precision spectroscopy requires controlling the temperature of the prism.</li><li>The small-angle formula n = 1 + A/2D is an approximation that fails beyond about 10 degrees of deviation; real work uses the exact minimum-deviation relation.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Typical prism apex angle</td><td>60 degrees</td></tr><tr><td>Primary rainbow angle</td><td>about 42 degrees</td></tr><tr><td>Dispersive power of crown glass</td><td>about 0.016</td></tr><tr><td>Refractive index of diamond</td><td>about 2.42</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How do modern observatories push spectroscopy to fainter objects than the classical prism or grating allowed?</b></p><p class="status">Engineering frontier</p><p>Very large telescopes, échelle spectrographs and interferometry have extended the reach, and each gain is limited by photon collection and detector noise.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can a dispersive element be built that disperses without deviating, or deviates without dispersing?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Compound and metamaterial designs approach these limits, but causality constrains how well an achromatic, deviation-free element can behave over a finite bandwidth.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Given the deviation angles of red and violet light through a 60 degree prism, find the two refractive indices and hence the dispersive power.</li><li>Measure the angle of minimum deviation for a triangular glass prism, compute n, and repeat with a hollow prism filled with water.</li><li>Explain geometrically why the deviation of a prism does not grow in proportion to the thickness of the glass.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 3</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/science-overview/science-explainers/spectroscopy-101-types-of-spectra-and-spectroscopy/">NASA: Spectroscopy 101</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/1-5-dispersion">OpenStax: Dispersion</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/4-4-diffraction-gratings">OpenStax: Diffraction Gratings</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
